{"name":"Young Green Onion","hex":"#aacf53","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"young","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["In the early part of growth or life; born not long ago.","At an early stage of existence or development; having recently come into existence."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Offspring, especially the immature offspring of animals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/young"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"onion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A monocotyledonous plant (Allium cepa), allied to garlic, used as vegetable and spice.","The bulb of such a plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/onion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Scallion","blocks":[{"text":"Scallions are edible vegetables of various species in the genus Allium. Scallions generally have a milder taste than most onions. Green onions/scallions have a long, delicate green stem that is white near the root. They have no bulb and a mild, sweet onion flavour. Their close relatives include garlic, shallots, leeks, chives, and Chinese onions. The leaves are eaten both raw and cooked."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/CSA-Red-Spring-Onions.jpg/330px-CSA-Red-Spring-Onions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scallion"},{"title":"Onion","blocks":[{"text":"The onion, also known as the bulb onion or common onion, is a vegetable that is the most widely cultivated species of the genus Allium. The shallot is a botanical variety of the onion which was classified as a separate species until 2011. The onion's close relatives include garlic, scallion, leek, and chives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Mixed_onions.jpg/330px-Mixed_onions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onion"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Germinating_scallion.jpg/500px-Germinating_scallion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Germinating_scallion.jpg/500px-Germinating_scallion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A germinating scallion, 10 days old","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGerminating_scallion.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/2010-06-19-supermarkt-by-RalfR-32.jpg/500px-2010-06-19-supermarkt-by-RalfR-32.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/2010-06-19-supermarkt-by-RalfR-32.jpg/1280px-2010-06-19-supermarkt-by-RalfR-32.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/2010-06-19-supermarkt-by-RalfR-32.jpg/500px-2010-06-19-supermarkt-by-RalfR-32.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A close-up view of spring onions (note the larger bulbs)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2010-06-19-supermarkt-by-RalfR-32.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Chopped_scallions.jpg/500px-Chopped_scallions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Chopped_scallions.jpg/1280px-Chopped_scallions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Chopped_scallions.jpg/500px-Chopped_scallions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chopped scallions","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChopped_scallions.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Haemul-pajeon_3.jpg/500px-Haemul-pajeon_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Haemul-pajeon_3.jpg/1280px-Haemul-pajeon_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Haemul-pajeon_3.jpg/500px-Haemul-pajeon_3.jpg?utm_source